445
00:44:22,980 --> 00:44:25,640
They worked hard, that's more important.

446
00:44:26,070 --> 00:44:29,170
We brought the community to the presidential.

447
00:44:29,170 --> 00:44:36,990
he didn't make serious mistakes in those very tricky days in 1967-69.

448
00:44:36,990 --> 00:44:40,990
Oh, and the University of California, much easier administration.

449
00:44:40,990 --> 00:44:43,990
Nobody brighter than Claude Coeur in Berkeley,

450
00:44:43,990 --> 00:44:48,990
and Meyerson, who is now, Martin, who is now President of the University of Pennsylvania.

451
00:44:48,990 --> 00:44:51,990
They were absolutely totally incomplicated,

452
00:44:51,990 --> 00:44:56,480
because they had lost all track of time.

453
00:44:56,480 --> 00:44:57,480
They had no board.

454
00:44:57,480 --> 00:45:07,480
The regents are, you know, the regents are ranked above the choir of our angels and communicate with you in the same fashion.

455
00:45:12,480 --> 00:45:20,480
But you have to work on your board. It's your job to think through what is the task of the board.

456
00:45:20,480 --> 00:45:25,480
You don't have a board that doesn't have a work program that you have written.

457
00:45:25,480 --> 00:45:29,480
Now, they have to accept it, but you have to develop.

458
00:45:29,480 --> 00:45:34,480
If not, you have got the choice between a board that's incompetent,

459
00:45:34,480 --> 00:45:42,130
that will let you down in a crisis, or a board that becomes busy and like Ted's board.

460
00:45:42,130 --> 00:45:47,130
Ted's board doesn't interfere with what he's doing for a very simple reason.

461
00:45:47,130 --> 00:45:52,130
They make damn sure that he can't do anything by tying up all his time.

462
00:45:52,130 --> 00:45:58,130
Well, then you don't have to interfere with what that is doing because you can't do anything.

463
00:45:58,130 --> 00:46:10,900
But that, you think through what is the function of your board, what is the task, what do you really expect of them.

464
00:46:10,900 --> 00:46:33,800
And then you look at your board, who are the few people in the board who want to come to you, and to whom you have a rapport, and you sit down with them and you say, look, you have been meeting every day of the week for three hours every night.

465
00:46:33,800 --> 00:46:45,700
You know, I sat on the Montclair State Board and on the Board of Higher Education in Jersey State, and the Montclair State Board met every week.

466
00:46:45,700 --> 00:46:53,770
It was a new board, just built the state college system. They went every week from 5 to midnight, one evening.

467
00:46:53,770 --> 00:47:01,770
And all we ever did for three years was to vote on maternity leave for Spanish instructors.

468
00:47:01,770 --> 00:47:10,770
And I said to that very tough guy who represented the State Board, who sat in on our meeting,

469
00:47:10,770 --> 00:47:13,770
do we have to do that bullshit?

470
00:47:13,770 --> 00:47:16,430
And she said, yes.

471
00:47:16,430 --> 00:47:19,920
And I won. I was chairman of the board.

472
00:47:19,920 --> 00:47:21,920
I won a hard fought battle.

473
00:47:21,920 --> 00:47:25,920
We didn't have to have those poor guys in to testify.

474
00:47:25,920 --> 00:47:28,920
Originally they had to come in and I think,

475
00:47:28,920 --> 00:47:35,920
What they expected us to do was to ask him to unrobe and see whether they were pregnant or not.

476
00:47:35,920 --> 00:47:40,710
I don't know. And they said, what happens if we don't vote?

477
00:47:40,710 --> 00:47:47,040
And she said, well, the state laws say she gets maternity leave anyhow.

478
00:47:47,040 --> 00:47:56,040
It took me three years of hard work to get permission to vote on all of them in one motion.

479
00:47:56,040 --> 00:48:03,440
We wasted all our time in the important things we didn't do.

480
00:48:03,440 --> 00:48:06,440
Terribly difficult things, important.

481
00:48:06,440 --> 00:48:11,440
The President needed us on curriculum, on direction, on faculty, on organization.

482
00:48:11,440 --> 00:48:14,440
We were willing to work hard, but he didn't let us,

483
00:48:14,440 --> 00:48:19,610
because he didn't think through what we were supposed to do.

484
00:48:19,610 --> 00:48:23,610
But we finally took the risk, and I think that we did.

485
00:48:23,610 --> 00:48:26,170
At that time, I was no longer chairman.

486
00:48:26,170 --> 00:48:33,170
The chairman was one of the evilest black men I've ever worked with, wonderful man,

487
00:48:33,170 --> 00:48:36,170
who was a physicist, physical chemist by lab.

488
00:48:36,170 --> 00:48:40,170
He and I kind of rammed it through.

489
00:48:40,170 --> 00:48:45,170
And another black girl who is now superintendent of schools in Hartford,

490
00:48:45,170 --> 00:48:49,670
Edith Gaines, wonderful, toughest gal I've ever worked with.

491
00:48:49,670 --> 00:48:54,670
The damn grads are not the subordinates.

492
00:48:54,670 --> 00:48:57,670
No danger, she's never hired them.

493
00:48:57,670 --> 00:49:04,510
It's really rammed in through.

494
00:49:04,510 --> 00:49:07,510
Then suddenly they're voting in the country.

495
00:49:07,510 --> 00:49:11,510
A board needs a work program and objectives

496
00:49:11,510 --> 00:49:16,510
and a clear understanding of what it is supposed to be doing.

497
00:49:16,510 --> 00:49:18,510
And then one sits down and says,

498
00:49:18,510 --> 00:49:22,510
look, you want 97 committees on the board

499
00:49:22,510 --> 00:49:31,960
because you want to keep everybody happy. The Boy Scout system of administration, there are three systems of administration in the world.

500
00:49:31,960 --> 00:49:42,920
One is the Chinese, the only one that works, which is don't tell anybody anything and keep them divided.

501
00:49:42,920 --> 00:49:52,920
Everybody, that was how Henry Louis spent time, life, and fortune, and nobody could figure out why, because he grew up in China.

502
00:49:52,920 --> 00:49:58,920
That's very much how FDR ran its administration, and on its mother's side, it's the China family.

503
00:49:58,920 --> 00:50:01,920
The Delenos were China's fates.

504
00:50:01,920 --> 00:50:05,920
Don't ever tell them anything, and keep them divided.

505
00:50:05,920 --> 00:50:08,550
That's the Chinese system.

506
00:50:08,550 --> 00:50:16,050
That's a language you have to keep in control, without the absentee management.

507
00:50:16,050 --> 00:50:19,050
It also limits what you can do.

508
00:50:19,050 --> 00:50:21,050
The other one is the Boycott system.

509
00:50:21,050 --> 00:50:25,050
Put everybody on four committees and you can't do any harm.

510
00:50:25,050 --> 00:50:32,670
On one condition, that those committees do not meet with the chief executives present.

511
00:50:34,540 --> 00:50:39,740
As long as they meet, you know, by having a committee, I know damn well they aren't going to do anything.

512
00:50:40,960 --> 00:50:45,680
The risk, you know, of a committee ever coming to a decision is small,

513
00:50:47,270 --> 00:50:52,730
provided the loan puts one of those widows on, who have nothing else to do.

514
00:50:52,730 --> 00:51:02,500
But if you have a tradition that the team will get to know the management after the

515
00:51:02,500 --> 00:51:08,730
president has committed, then that system needs to be spent all the time, taking in

516
00:51:08,730 --> 00:51:17,730
meetings that discuss this trivia, such as whether to replace the Dorianda tree on Passway

517
00:51:17,730 --> 00:51:20,590
8 that hasn't had flowers for four years.

518
00:51:21,750 --> 00:51:22,270
All right.

519
00:51:22,390 --> 00:51:27,990
You know, you get around to it when you get around to Passway 8, next year, year after.

520
00:51:27,990 --> 00:51:29,150
And then so what?

521
00:51:29,210 --> 00:51:43,860
only has leaves and no flowers. Committees like trivia because you never need a decision

522
00:51:43,860 --> 00:51:50,090
and you can keep on talking forever about trivia. So they always like it. And maybe

523
00:51:50,090 --> 00:51:58,120
you settle that by having everybody on a committee and lay down the rule that each committee

524
00:51:58,120 --> 00:52:05,450
twice a year reports to the committee of the whole and then you are present. Then you prevent

525
00:52:05,450 --> 00:52:15,030
and otherwise they need, without being influenced by that malevolent influence of the chief executive,

526
00:52:15,030 --> 00:52:18,030
who otherwise would try to dominate them.

527
00:52:18,030 --> 00:52:23,980
Oh, and then encourage committees, but the moment they need you to be present,

528
00:52:23,980 --> 00:52:28,980
realize that they don't do any work and won't allow you to do any.

529
00:52:28,980 --> 00:52:36,380
And the sales management system is a management, which so far exists only in books, you know.

530
00:52:36,380 --> 00:52:46,460
What we have in reality is either Boy Scouts or Chinese. But you do need an effective force.

531
00:52:46,460 --> 00:52:51,460
You need it because every organization sooner or later gets into trouble.

532
00:52:51,460 --> 00:53:00,340
And then you need somebody who can say to the community, lay off these people, they are trying to do a job.

533
00:53:00,340 --> 00:53:06,960
You need it because in every organization you have a succession crisis.

534
00:53:06,960 --> 00:53:14,650
and accept the fact that they're strong as a leader, they're more difficult to success in Christ.

535
00:53:14,650 --> 00:53:21,300
Strong leaders do not breed strong successors. They breed a system.

536
00:53:21,300 --> 00:53:22,300
Yes, Mr. McKenzie.

537
00:53:22,300 --> 00:53:25,540
Yes, inside port and outside port.

538
00:53:25,540 --> 00:53:32,130
Well, you need an outside port, basically. An inside port is just a management committee.

539
00:53:32,130 --> 00:53:40,100
It's just a legally sanctioned form of instance.

540
00:53:40,100 --> 00:53:47,520
I can't hear you.

541
00:53:47,520 --> 00:53:52,340
You were saying something about strong leaders bring what?

542
00:53:52,340 --> 00:53:55,830
Bring the succession crisis.

543
00:53:55,830 --> 00:54:01,830
Every book tells you that strong leaders should raise strong successors,

544
00:54:01,830 --> 00:54:06,710
and it happened very rarely in the history of the world.

545
00:54:06,710 --> 00:54:12,800
It is not impossible.

546
00:54:12,800 --> 00:54:15,800
But Julius Caesar did not raise Augustus.

547
00:54:15,800 --> 00:54:20,200
Augustus, if Augustus had, if Julius Caesar hadn't been assassinated,

548
00:54:20,380 --> 00:54:21,820
there wouldn't have been an Augustus.

549
00:54:22,480 --> 00:54:23,940
There would have been a Morganton.

550
00:54:27,300 --> 00:54:30,800
And Augustus left behind, who was the next one?

551
00:54:30,820 --> 00:54:31,140
Nero?

552
00:54:32,730 --> 00:54:33,450
Yeah, wasn't it?

553
00:54:34,400 --> 00:54:34,820
What?

554
00:54:35,380 --> 00:54:35,440
Yeah.

555
00:54:36,900 --> 00:54:37,420
Tiberius.

556
00:54:37,420 --> 00:54:37,520
Tiberius.

557
00:54:37,640 --> 00:54:38,600
It's in Nero.

558
00:54:39,320 --> 00:54:39,720
What?

559
00:54:40,840 --> 00:54:41,860
Calvius and then Nero.

560
00:54:42,280 --> 00:54:44,000
Calvius didn't last very long.

561
00:54:44,160 --> 00:54:45,200
But, you know, Tiberius.

562
00:54:45,200 --> 00:55:02,420
And it is one of the miracles of political history that the Roman Republic, the Roman Empire, had a succession of leaders beginning with Tyon in 78 B.C. A.D.

563
00:55:02,420 --> 00:55:16,400
and ending with Marcus Aurelius a hundred years later, where you had five successors, where each leader bred and raised a strong successor.

564
00:55:16,400 --> 00:55:44,100
That's one of the miracles. There's no other. And it is not totally impossible. And one of the reasons why George Marshall is one of my heroes is that he did it. And consciously. He wasn't afraid of them on the one hand. And he also restrained himself. But it's very rare.

565
00:55:44,100 --> 00:56:00,650
And if you're left behind a vacuum, which the Democratic Party hasn't filled yet, if you don't mind by saying so, that is typical of the strong leaders.

566
00:56:00,650 --> 00:56:12,960
and then you need a board that can move and can make sure that the strong leaders,

567
00:56:12,960 --> 00:56:16,960
Chief Turk doesn't get to be successor, no.

568
00:56:16,960 --> 00:56:24,780
Strong leaders look for carbon copies and carbon copies are weak.

569
00:56:24,780 --> 00:56:30,780
Or they look for helpers who have never made a decision

570
00:56:30,780 --> 00:56:33,780
because they always relied on the strength of the strong man.

571
00:56:33,780 --> 00:56:50,240
That's typical. Then you need a board. And then let me say there is no way in which you can know in advance whether somebody will work out in the top spot or top spot.

572
00:56:50,240 --> 00:57:03,110
There is no way, partly because no chief executive, whether you are talking of the American president or of the ABC grocery store,

573
00:57:03,110 --> 00:57:12,110
had ever come in and been faced by the contingencies which he prepared for.

574
00:57:12,110 --> 00:57:23,830
Every American president since George Washington has found within a year of taking over that his real challenges were not what he prepared for.

575
00:57:23,830 --> 00:57:29,830
Jefferson came in absolutely determined to be concerned only with domestic affairs.

576
00:57:29,830 --> 00:57:53,690
Well, you know, Napoleon didn't allow him that luxury, as the British, and if there ever was a president who had no interest in foreign affairs in American history, it was Lyndon Johnson. Lyndon Johnson thought that he was already exceedingly cosmopolitan by being interested in what went on in Arkansas, beyond what was on...

577
00:57:53,690 --> 00:58:12,730
No, a great man, we don't write him off, as a truly great man, totally defeated by a world he didn't make and by loyalty to the Kennedy members.

578
00:58:12,730 --> 00:58:24,190
What defeated him, Johnson said he couldn't fire those goddamn Kennedy survivors who had no respect for a redneck from Texas because he didn't have a Ph.D.

579
00:58:24,750 --> 00:58:26,590
Those damn arrogant snobs.

580
00:58:27,350 --> 00:58:29,710
And he should have fired every one of them.

581
00:58:29,710 --> 00:58:39,600
Day two, not day three, day one all right, but day two you're fired, and bring in people who were sick.

582
00:58:39,600 --> 00:58:44,600
And he didn't leave. He had that tremendous labyrinth of the Kennedy Memorial.

583
00:58:44,600 --> 00:58:47,600
And they betrayed him, they sold him down the river.

584
00:58:47,600 --> 00:58:59,680
So, in it, there were the challenges of a major war. Vietnam War was a major war, thanks to the Kennedy legacy.

585
00:58:59,680 --> 00:59:08,930
John Kennedy made it, if you want, totally empty. And the dollar crisis and a few other things.

586
00:59:08,930 --> 00:59:18,340
He came to the presidency determined to finish off the U.D. domestically, and he did that.

587
00:59:18,340 --> 00:59:29,980
But that's what the U.S. is. And that is typical. And Johnson's great weakness was that he didn't exist.

588
00:59:29,980 --> 00:59:50,820
Truman's great strength was that he did, that he did not say this is what I set out to do, but this is what the situation demanded. And so a very mediocre Truman became a very substantial president, not a very big one, and a giant.
